WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.620
- What?

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:03.380
- The computer was from a woman?

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:04.310
Did you know this?

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:07.150
Women are so cool.

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:08.240
Hey, what's up y'all?

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.150
So you know I love a lot of woman,

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:11.620
I even have a song about it, okay?

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:13.890
Get ready to be inspired and
maybe learn something new

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.030
because these next few minutes are going

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:19.080
to be completely dedicated to
one of my favorite F-words.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:20.060
Females.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:21.220
Get your mind out of the gutter.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:22.490
It's Women's History Month,

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:25.490
a time to celebrate trailblazing
women throughout history,

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:28.020
like Harriet Tubman,
Amelia Earhart, Rosa Parks,

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:30.550
RBG, Ruth Bader Ginsburg, Michelle Obama.

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:31.470
They're all so inspiring.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:32.530
There's so many women,

00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:35.220
but today we're going to
look at some incredible women

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:36.780
who you may not know,

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:39.280
but you do know what
these women have done.

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:41.400
So let's meet some women
who changed the world

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:42.420
with their inventions.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:43.641
Here we go.

00:00:43.641 --> 00:00:46.224
(upbeat music)

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:47.983
I'm actually excited,

00:00:47.983 --> 00:00:49.030
but I don't know some of these things

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:49.863
that are probably gonna come up.

00:00:49.863 --> 00:00:51.300
So I am on the ride with you.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:52.214
Drum roll please.

00:00:52.214 --> 00:00:53.047
(trills tongue)

00:00:53.047 --> 00:00:53.880
Oh, thank you.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:54.713
Oh, what did I do?

00:00:54.713 --> 00:00:55.546
What was I doing?

00:00:55.546 --> 00:00:57.009
Was I making a fire, like what?

00:00:57.009 --> 00:00:59.880
(phone rings)

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:01.280
Caller ID?

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.500
Of course a woman made up Caller ID.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.930
She was like, I'm screening these calls.

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:07.230
I don't wanna talk to him.

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:08.850
You can thank Shirley for that,

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.980
'cause Shirley Ann Jackson is
a modern day female inventor

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:14.300
who was the first black woman to graduate

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:18.080
with her PhD from MIT in 1973.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:20.230
That's amazing.

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:23.270
She completed research that
has since led to such creations

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:26.910
as the portable fax machine,
touch tone telephones,

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:29.390
solar cells, and yep, Caller ID,

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:30.562
which we love.

00:01:30.562 --> 00:01:32.087
(upbeat music)

00:01:32.087 --> 00:01:33.863
(drum roll)

00:01:33.863 --> 00:01:36.410
Windshield wipers, seriously?

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:41.400
In 1903, entrepreneur Mary
Anderson saw that tram drivers,

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:44.040
that's how people got around
before cars were mainstream,

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:45.280
tram drivers.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.330
Tram drivers would lean out of the tram

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:51.430
in order to wipe the windshield
in the rain and snow.

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:52.540
Oh my gosh.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:55.040
So Mary patented the first-ever design

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.400
for the windshield wiper
with a rubber blade

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:00.920
and spring arm that would
wipe the windshield itself.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:02.470
Way to go.

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:05.530
Cadillac was the first car
manufacturer to adopt them

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:08.350
as standard equipment in 1922.

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:12.000
However, Anderson never
profited from her invention

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.393
because her patent ran out in 1920.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.879
I have so many comments right now,

00:02:18.879 --> 00:02:21.140
but I'm gonna keep 'em to myself.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.150
I'm very mad about that.

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:24.390
That is so not fair.

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:26.640
At least her family
should get the residuals.

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:29.000
Okay.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:30.500
That's my reaction to that.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:32.930
So every time I flick on
my wipers from now on,

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:35.149
I will say thank you, Mary Anderson.

00:02:35.149 --> 00:02:37.732
(techno music)

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:39.830
What?

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:41.550
The computer was from a woman.

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:43.530
Did you know this?

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:45.270
When you think about modern computers,

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:47.020
you probably think Steve
Jobs and Bill Gates.

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:47.853
Yeah?

00:02:47.853 --> 00:02:50.580
But really it was Grace Hopper
along with Howard Aiken,

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:54.960
who designed Harvard's
Mark One computer in 1944,

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:56.450
which filled an entire room,

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:57.420
I did know it was huge,

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:58.450
and weighed five tons.

00:02:58.450 --> 00:02:59.360
I did know it was huge,

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:02.000
but the first ever computer,

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:03.173
a woman was a part of that?

00:03:03.173 --> 00:03:04.540
I didn't know that.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:05.530
That's really rad.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:07.460
Hopper invented the compiler

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:08.880
that translated written language

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:13.620
into computer code and coined
the term bug and debugging

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:18.620
when she literally had to
remove moths from the device.

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:21.360
Oh my gosh.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.990
Grace Hopper, first computer.

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:25.720
Thank you, Grace Hopper.

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:27.050
And once again, Mary Anderson,

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:29.130
because you never got credit for it,

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:31.870
and I'm pretty upset about
that whole thing still.

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:34.130
We didn't have like a
computer 'cause we were poor,

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:36.210
but my mom did get a word processor,

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:37.070
if you remember what that was?

00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:38.610
And I thought it was the coolest thing,

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:39.810
and I'd pretend to sit at it

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:41.590
like I was writing my report for college.

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:43.080
Like I would, I, yeah,

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:44.810
I was a nerd and still am.

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:46.430
And remember the Oregon Trail?

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:48.150
Everyone died of dysentery.

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:49.150
Yeah. Yeah.

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:49.983
It's a fun game.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:53.710
Real uplifter family event,

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:55.205
you all died of dysentery.

00:03:55.205 --> 00:03:58.530
(triumphant trumpet music)

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:00.060
The dishwasher.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:01.640
Well, you know why woman made that up?

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:02.473
She was done.

00:04:02.473 --> 00:04:03.306
Yeah.

00:04:03.306 --> 00:04:06.190
In 1872, Josephine Cochran

00:04:06.190 --> 00:04:08.840
had what we would describe
as a basic problem.

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:10.530
Her dishes would often get broken

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:12.450
by whomever was washing them.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:13.530
That's what you're trying to get your kids

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:15.090
to do some work in your house,

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:16.020
and that didn't happen.

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:17.590
So with the help of an engineer,

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:20.600
she designed the first-ever
hands-free dishwasher,

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:22.570
which would use pressurized jets

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:25.290
of soapy water to do the cleaning.

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:27.200
Genius.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:28.130
That is amazing,

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:29.800
and I'm very thankful Josephine,

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:32.020
'cause that is a time saver.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:32.853
I was like,

00:04:32.853 --> 00:04:33.760
but you know what?

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:35.370
You gotta still clean the food off,

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:36.580
or it's just gonna be in there.

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:38.040
That's so gross.

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:40.170
Like just clean it off and put
in the dishwasher disposal.

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:41.760
Like not dishwasher, dish.

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:42.593
I can't talk.

00:04:42.593 --> 00:04:45.320
Just clean it off and
put it in the disposal.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:46.595
Nope. Dispose...

00:04:46.595 --> 00:04:47.930
(woman laughs)

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:50.923
This next female invention
could cause a controversy.

00:04:51.882 --> 00:04:52.715
Beer.

00:04:52.715 --> 00:04:53.970
(dramatic music)

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:55.330
Beer?

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:56.720
Listen up beer lovers.

00:04:56.720 --> 00:04:58.780
Next time you chug your favorite brew,

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:01.270
you might wanna cheers
the women around you.

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:02.103
Because why?

00:05:02.103 --> 00:05:05.120
Historians say the oldest
evidence of beer making

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:08.550
is seen roughly 7,000 years ago?

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:10.480
Well they even needed
escapism then, didn't they?

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:13.250
And it was female Sumerians

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:15.623
who first discovered the process.

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:17.870
That's so cool.

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:20.830
They even have a goddess of beer,

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:22.750
known as Ninkasi?

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:24.767
Cheers to that.

00:05:24.767 --> 00:05:28.860
(triumphant trumpet music)

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:31.340
Wireless transmission technology.

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:34.400
Okay wait, you may know Hedy
Lamar as a black and white,

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:36.600
oh I, oh, I knew this one.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:37.620
I didn't remember that I knew it,

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:38.760
so maybe I didn't know it.

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:40.440
As a black and white Hollywood movie star

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:41.760
in the thirties, forties, fifties

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:42.970
known for playing Delilah

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:46.280
in Cecil B. DeMille's Sampson and Delilah.

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:47.230
Hedy Lamar right there.

00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:48.650
You know how I knew this?

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:52.340
I feel like I saw this in
an episode of Timeless.

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:53.810
I feel like that's how I knew this,

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:55.030
was from a TV show.

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:57.110
But did y'all know that
during World War II,

00:05:57.110 --> 00:06:01.530
this movie star also worked
to improve torpedo technology.

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:03.950
So as a gifted mathematician and engineer,

00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:06.100
she worked with a music composer

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:08.170
to develop frequency hopping,

00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:10.450
which would encrypt
torpedo control signals

00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:12.930
so the enemy couldn't
send them off course.

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:14.380
But even more impressive,

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:16.530
if that's not impressive enough,

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:18.270
the type of technology she developed

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:20.170
ended up being the foundation

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:22.760
for many modern day inventions,

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:26.060
including wifi and GPS.

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:27.310
That's so cool.

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:28.360
Hope she got paid.

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:30.300
Yeah, exactly.

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:31.900
Mary Anderson didn't.

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:32.733
Remember her.

00:06:32.733 --> 00:06:34.686
Mary Anderson, windshield wipers.

00:06:34.686 --> 00:06:37.720
(upbeat music)

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:40.540
Bulletproof fiber for
like bulletproof vests?

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:42.210
Women are so cool.

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:43.390
Wow.

00:06:43.390 --> 00:06:46.240
In 1965, Stephanie, am
I gonna say this right?

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:47.073
Kwolek?

00:06:47.073 --> 00:06:47.906
- [Crew Member] Mm-hmm.

00:06:47.906 --> 00:06:48.900
An American chemist was trying

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:51.690
to create a new lightweight
material for car tires,

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:53.700
'cause she's already
rad trying to do that,

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:56.550
when she accidentally
invented a brand new material,

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:57.860
Kevlar.

00:06:57.860 --> 00:06:58.870
What?

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:00.550
The new material was lightweight,

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:02.500
five times stronger than steel,

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:06.000
and was eventually used to
create bulletproof vests.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:07.690
That is so cool.

00:07:07.690 --> 00:07:10.430
It's now also used in boats, airplanes,

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:12.800
and in total about 200 applications.

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:14.370
Go, Stephanie.

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:16.290
I did not know that women
invented those things.

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:17.380
I hope you learned something

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:19.110
that you loved here today as well.

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:21.500
All right, well happy Women's
History Month everybody.

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:24.330
Let's continue to change
the world as we do.

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:25.163
Bye.

